{- | Decision-matrix gate for `docs/spec/SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md`, over
   `test/shadow_fixtures`.

   The corpus is that spec's enforcement — one fixture per matrix cell — and
   the table below is the roster of what each cell does. Clause S7 specifies
   that `check`, `run` and `build` agree on every cell, so agreement between
   them is not evidence: a shadow that resolves to the wrong binding resolves
   the same wrong way on all three paths, and an exit-code gate would grade
   that green. Every cell that can accept therefore also pins the exact
   stdout the program owes.

   The table pins what the binary does, cell by cell, and each row's `label`
   is load-bearing. A row carries why its verdict is what it is and which
   fix moved it, because several rows are numerically indistinguishable from
   an outcome that was a bug. `d21` pins `4\n3`, byte-identical to `d11`'s
   old bug output and the opposite verdict: `d11`'s `4` was an unqualified
   call the impl universe stole, `d21`'s is dispatch the author requested by
   writing `Ix a i =>`. A reader who transcribes the verdicts and paraphrases
   the labels destroys the pin.

   `i1`, `i3`, `i4` and `i5` are importer shadows and must not move. The S2
   inversion is confined to definer shadows — an `import` is a sibling scope,
   not an inner one — and inverting importers would break the everyday
   `import map` pattern. If one of those four moves, the inversion has leaked.

   When adding a fixture, vary the receiver's provenance (literal, grounded,
   dict-bound), not just its type: that axis is where every silent bug in
   this area has lived, and a gate that cannot express a cell cannot defend
   it. The coverage assertion below refuses a fixture that is present in the
   corpus but absent from the table, which is the orphan-corpus failure this
   gate exists to close.

   Three value modes are live. A future known-bad row needing a different
   one adds a `Mode` constructor and an arm in `gradeValue`: the shell gate
   this replaced also carried `BUILD_CRASH` (build exits 0 and the shipped
   binary dies with a pinned signature — exit 139 and `E-FATAL-SIGNAL` on
   stderr — while `run` prints the pinned value) and `BUILD_NOTEQ_INT`
   (build's stdout is a bare integer that is *not* the pinned value, for a
   nondeterministic garbage-pointer miscompile). Both drained; both are
   re-expressible here unchanged, because `Agreement` keeps the built
   binary's exit code, stdout and stderr apart. A ledger row must pin the
   signature and not merely "nonzero", so that it goes red both when the bug
   is fixed and when the binary starts failing for a different reason.

   The per-cell verdict table is rendered into the failure message rather
   than printed: `medaka test --native` runs a test body inside a probe whose
   stdout it parses back as a sentinel transcript, so anything the body
   prints on its own account is absorbed there and never reaches a reader. A
   red run therefore names every disagreeing cell and its columns at once.

   Runs under `medaka test --native`: it spawns subprocesses and reads the
   corpus, neither of which the interpreter binds. -}

import compiler_cli_test_support.{
  Agreement(..), binaryRanClean, checkRunBuild, sweepUnits, verdictOf
}
import fs.{fixtureDirs}
import list.{somes, sort}
import path.{basename}
import string.{endsWith, padRight, replaceAll, stripSuffix, unlines}
import test.{Expectation, fail, pass}
import test_process.{medakaRoot, unrosteredUnits}

-- Fixtures are located through MEDAKA_ROOT rather than the working
-- directory: `medaka gate run` exports it and inherits whatever cwd the
-- caller had.
corpus : <IO> String
corpus = "\{medakaRoot}/test/shadow_fixtures"

{- | What a row asserts about the stdout of a cell that runs.

   `NoValue` is for a cell all three verbs reject, where nothing ran.
   `AllExact` requires the run engine's stdout, the built binary's stdout
   and the pinned value to be byte-identical. `BuildExact` asserts only the
   built binary's stdout, for a cell `run` rejects by design while `build`
   accepts. -}
data Mode = NoValue | AllExact | BuildExact

{- | One matrix cell: the entry path under the corpus, the label carrying
   why the cell reads as it does, the verdict each verb owes, and what the
   cell prints. -}
data Row = Row {
  entry : String,
  label : String,
  expCheck : String,
  expRun : String,
  expBuild : String,
  mode : Mode,
  pinned : String,
}

table : List Row
table = [
  Row {
    entry = "d1b_definer_noimpl_zeroimpls.mdk",
    label = "D1b definer, iface has ZERO impls (S2)",
    expCheck = "ACCEPT",
    expRun = "ACCEPT",
    expBuild = "ACCEPT",
    mode = AllExact,
    pinned = "4\n",
  },
  Row {
    entry = "d1_definer_noimpl.mdk",
    label = "D1 definer, no-impl receiver, impl exists elsewhere (S2)",
    expCheck = "ACCEPT",
    expRun = "ACCEPT",
    expBuild = "ACCEPT",
    mode = AllExact,
    pinned = "4\n",
  },
  Row {
